/- 3. Let $x$ be a real number, $y=|x-1|+|x-1|$. Among the following four conclusions:

I . $y$ has no minimum value;
II. There is only one $x$ that makes $y$ reach its minimum value;
III. There are a finite number of $x$ (more than one) that make $y$ reach its maximum value;
$\mathbb{N}$. There are infinitely many $x$ that make $y$ reach its minimum value.
The correct one is
(A) I ;
(B) $\mathbb{I I}$;
(C) III ;
(D) $\mathrm{N}$. -/
theorem algebra_111845 {y : ℝ → ℝ} (hy : ∀ x, y x = abs (x - 1) + abs (x - 1)) :
    IsLeast (range y) (y 1) ∧ ¬∃! x, y x = y 1 ∧ ¬∃ n, {x | y x = y 1}.encard = n := by

